## B13. ERROR HANDLING AND EDGE CASES

Create a central, user-friendly error handling system. Never expose raw stack traces, API keys, file paths containing private information, or database internals in ordinary UI messages. Save diagnostic logs safely and offer a “Copy diagnostic details” action with secrets redacted.

Explicitly handle:
- Database locked, corrupt, missing, or migration failure.
- Disk full and permission denied.
- Missing Python interpreter or unsupported Python version.
- Code execution timeout, excessive output, process crash, or sandbox unavailable.
- AI provider authentication failure, quota exhaustion, rate limit, network loss, malformed response, or unsupported model.
- Invalid/corrupt lesson content and missing assets.
- Broken YouTube/documentation links.
- Backup interrupted, restore conflict, and incompatible backup version.
- User closes the application during a write, exam, or code execution.
- Duplicate username/profile and invalid input.
- Window resized below preferred dimensions.

Every error message should explain what happened in plain Uzbek, what the user can do next, whether their data is safe, and whether retrying is appropriate. Log technical detail separately. Never silently discard user work.

## B14. PERFORMANCE, RELIABILITY AND DATA PROTECTION

- Keep UI responsive; long operations run outside the UI thread.
- Show cancellable progress for lengthy imports, backups, content validation, AI calls, and packaging tasks.
- Use pagination/lazy loading for large course catalogs, histories, and logs.
- Avoid loading every lesson, image, and chart on startup.
- Cache only data that can be safely regenerated and invalidate it correctly.
- Use database transactions and migrations; test interrupted writes and recovery.
- Back up before destructive migrations or major content upgrades.
- Do not lose learner progress when lessons are revised, reordered, or renamed.
- Keep profile data isolated at service/repository level, not merely by hiding UI elements.
- Make offline behavior explicit: core downloaded lessons and local practice should remain available; AI and external video links may require internet.

## B15. ACCESSIBILITY AND LANGUAGE QUALITY

- All controls must have accessible names and keyboard focus indicators.
- Do not communicate status by color alone; pair color with text/icon/shape.
- Maintain readable contrast and scalable typography.
- Support keyboard navigation and logical tab order.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Use correct Uzbek Latin orthography consistently, including apostrophe characters and punctuation; avoid mixed spelling variants in the same interface.
- Keep translation keys centralized. No hardcoded UI strings.
- Ensure English mode translates all UI controls and messages, while lesson content availability is clearly labeled by language.
- If a lesson is not yet translated, do not silently display the wrong language; show a clear language availability notice and an explicit fallback option.

